llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
102 and 129): patch 102's decode kernel for head size 512, which Gemma 4
uses in its full-attention layers.
gfx803-specific: engages only for cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4 (host) and GCN4
(device code).

WHY
---
Gemma 4 26B-A4B has five full-attention layers with head size 512 and 16
query heads over 2 K/V heads; its other 25 layers use head size 256 over a
1024-token sliding window. The vec kernel has no instance for head size 512,
so with a q8_0 KV cache one query token of those five layers goes to the
tile kernel. That converts the whole K/V range to fp16 first and then runs
a tile instance that spills about 4 KB per lane to scratch. On an RX 590
(test-backend-ops perf, 2 K/V heads x 8 query heads) one layer takes:

    K/V length       512    2048    8192    32768
    tile kernel      635    1402    4529    16495 us

At 32k tokens of context that is 82 ms per generated token in those five
layers alone. Generation falls from 18.5 tok/s with ~2,000 tokens of
context to 10.6 tok/s at 19,500.

WHAT
----
ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode() also takes head size 512, and
ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext() tries it in the tile branch before the tile
kernel. The function's other conditions are unchanged (gfx803, one query
token, q8_0 K and V, GQA ratio 2 or more, a mask, a KV length that is a
multiple of 256). On gfx803 a quantized KV cache with head size 256 or less
already takes the vec branch for one query token, so head size 512 is the
only case this adds.

At head size 512 a lane holds 8 output dimensions per query head. Only the
2-head blocks stay within 128 VGPRs (115, against 134 and 209 at 4 and 8
heads, no spills), and the second wave per SIMD that allows is faster than
sharing each K/V row among more heads at every length:

    K/V length       512    2048    8192    32768
    8 heads/block    139     240     474     1723 us
    4 heads/block     77     142     434     1483 us
    2 heads/block     48      88     260      887 us

so head size 512 always runs 2 heads per block; head sizes 64-256 keep
patch 102's choice.

Result on an RX 590, Gemma 4 26B-A4B-it heretic-ara i1-Q4_K_M, experts in
system RAM, llama-server, q8_0 KV cache, greedy:

    eight ~2,000-token prompts, 300 tokens   18.53 -> 21.44 tok/s
    7,800-token prompt, 150 tokens           14.82 -> 20.80 tok/s
    19,500-token prompt, 150 tokens          10.56 -> 19.95 tok/s

Prompt processing is unchanged.

VERIFIED ON REAL HARDWARE (RX 590):
- test-backend-ops test -b ROCm0 -o FLASH_ATTN_EXT: all 604 q8_0 cases
  pass against the CPU backend, including 240 added one-token cases with
  q8_0 K/V: head sizes 256 and 512, 1, 2 and 4 K/V heads, GQA ratios 2, 3,
  4, 8 and 16, KV length 256-4096, with and without sinks.
- llama-perplexity KL divergence against the CPU backend with one-token
  micro-batches (-ub 1), so that every token runs this kernel: 8 chunks of
  512 tokens 0.436 before, 0.416 after (same top token 80.9% / 81.2%);
  2 chunks of 2048 tokens, which score positions past the 1024-token
  sliding window, 0.322 / 0.324 (82.3% / 82.4%). Two CPU code paths (with
  and without weight repacking) differ by 0.36 on this model.
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-gcn4-decode.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-gcn4-decode.cuh
index 12c2b01..cb3965d 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-gcn4-decode.cuh
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn-gcn4-decode.cuh
@@ -283,19 +283,28 @@ static void ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case(ggml_backend_cuda_context
 
 template <int D>
 static void ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_switch_ncols2(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, ggml_tensor * dst, const int gqa_ratio) {
-    // Wider head groups read K/V fewer times but give fewer, longer blocks, which only pays
-    // off once there are enough blocks to fill the GPU. The block counts at which a width
-    // starts to win were measured on an RX 590 (36 CUs) with 4 and 8 K/V heads.
-    const ggml_tensor * K = dst->src[1];
-    auto nblocks = [&](const int ncols2) {
-        return K->ne[2]*K->ne[3]*((gqa_ratio + ncols2 - 1)/ncols2)*(K->ne[1]/fattn_gcn4_rows);
-    };
-    if (gqa_ratio > 4 && nblocks(8) >= 128) {
-        ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 8>(ctx, dst);
-    } else if (gqa_ratio > 2 && nblocks(4) >= 32) {
-        ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 4>(ctx, dst);
-    } else {
+    if constexpr (D == 512) {
+        // A lane holds 8 output dimensions per query head here: the 2-head blocks fit in 128 VGPRs,
+        // and the second wave per SIMD that allows beats sharing K/V among more heads at every KV
+        // length (RX 590, 2 K/V heads x 8 query heads, 512-32768 keys: 48-889 us against 77-1483
+        // at 4 heads and 139-1723 at 8).
+        GGML_UNUSED(gqa_ratio);
         ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 2>(ctx, dst);
+    } else {
+        // Wider head groups read K/V fewer times but give fewer, longer blocks, which only pays
+        // off once there are enough blocks to fill the GPU. The block counts at which a width
+        // starts to win were measured on an RX 590 (36 CUs) with 4 and 8 K/V heads.
+        const ggml_tensor * K = dst->src[1];
+        auto nblocks = [&](const int ncols2) {
+            return K->ne[2]*K->ne[3]*((gqa_ratio + ncols2 - 1)/ncols2)*(K->ne[1]/fattn_gcn4_rows);
+        };
+        if (gqa_ratio > 4 && nblocks(8) >= 128) {
+            ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 8>(ctx, dst);
+        } else if (gqa_ratio > 2 && nblocks(4) >= 32) {
+            ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 4>(ctx, dst);
+        } else {
+            ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_case<D, 2>(ctx, dst);
+        }
     }
 }
 
@@ -331,6 +340,7 @@ static bool ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx
         case  64: ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_switch_ncols2< 64>(ctx, dst, gqa_ratio); return true;
         case 128: ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_switch_ncols2<128>(ctx, dst, gqa_ratio); return true;
         case 256: ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_switch_ncols2<256>(ctx, dst, gqa_ratio); return true;
+        case 512: ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode_switch_ncols2<512>(ctx, dst, gqa_ratio); return true;
         default:  return false;
     }
 }
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu
index 9eddea0..4336227 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu
@@ -763,6 +763,11 @@ void ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, ggml_tensor * dst
         case BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_NONE:
             GGML_ABORT("fatal error");
         case BEST_FATTN_KERNEL_TILE:
+            // The vec kernel has no instance for head size 512, so one query token of Gemma 4's global
+            // layers lands here.
+            if (ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_decode(ctx, dst)) {
+                break;
+            }
             if (!ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_gcn4_prompt(ctx, dst)) {
                 ggml_cuda_flash_attn_ext_tile(ctx, dst);
             }
